        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_614"/>
        <dc:title>On field recognition of pig cough based on multimodal audio representation and fusion with contrastive learning</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Cough is an early clinical sign of pig respiratory diseases, of which accurate and timely recognition is critical to early warning of pig respiratory diseases. Recent pig cough recognition methods suffered from limited label training samples and failed to obtain powerful audio representations. In this study, a novel multimodal pig audio representation and fusion (MSS) framework was proposed to learn meaningful feature representations. Based on the MSS framework, this study proposed a method for recognizing pig cough in practical production conditions. The results showed that the MSS framework demonstrated excellent capability of learning pig audio representations, based on which the pig cough recognition method achieved accurate results by fusing the mel-frequency cepstral coefficient (MFCC) and log-mel spectrogram, with an accuracy of 91.37 % using a simple linear classifier. After fine-tuning the supervision dataset, the performance of the pig cough recognition method was further improved to 94.32 % and outperformed the state-of-the-art supervised pig cough recognition methods. The proposed MSS framework effectively addressed the issue that the labeled training samples were limited in practical production conditions, enabling the proposed pig cough recognition method to maintain accuracy even with a 50 % reduction in training samples and to yield decent recognition results with approximately 3 % of the total cough samples. This study indicates that the proposed pig cough recognition method based on the MSS framework can effectively complement the supervised methods and make a promising tool for early warning of pig respiratory diseases.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>11/2025</dc:date>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
